# Core efficiency

- For non-trivial work: read `PROJECT_MEMORY.md` once at session start; do not re-dump it later.
- Prefer Ask or Plan before multi-file edits. One logical slice per chat when possible.
- Targeted reads and greps only. No whole-repo dumps. Prefer `/explorer-cheap` for discovery.
- Never store real credentials in the repo or chat. Local settings store only.
- No Jira writes / credential prompts until the user says a Jira account exists.
- Confirm before: security-level changes, deletes, credential updates, Jira writes.
- Prefer small diffs; extend existing plugins — do not create parallel systems.
- After a finished slice: update Current State in `PROJECT_MEMORY.md` if behaviour changed.

# Model selection (cheapest first)

- Always start with the cheapest capable model: **Composer 2.5** or **Auto** (if available). Gemini 2.5 Flash is also acceptable for simple work.
- Do not use Grok 4.7, Claude Opus, or other frontier models unless the cheaper model has failed twice on the same task, or the task is explicitly marked as requiring a stronger model (e.g. visual orb matching against reference images).
- If you escalate, state why in one sentence before switching.
- Prefer the model picker to stay on Composer/Auto unless escalation is justified.

# Git handling (agents do this, never ask the user)

- You have terminal access. Run all git commands yourself.
- At the start of a coding session: if not on `main`, stash any local changes, checkout `main`, and pull.
- Before committing: `git status` and `git diff`. Only commit the files you intentionally changed.
- Commit with a clear message, then `git push origin main` (or the current branch if explicitly working on one).
- Never ask the user to run git, stash, pull, or push. Do it yourself.
- If a pull would overwrite local work, stash first, pull, then decide whether to pop.

